WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.920
Close one eye of a young mouse for a few days, and its visual cortex answers that

2
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.960
eye much less. Do the same to an adult, and far less happens.

3
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.820
We can ask why in the mouse, because its genes can be changed.

4
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.240
So what opens the window?

5
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.020
Is it simply age?

6
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:29.033
A knockout mouse lacks a working copy of one gene.

7
00:00:30.013 --> 00:00:36.933
Mice make GABA with two enzymes, and one of them, GAD65, supplies GABA when neurons

8
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:43.453
fire hard. A knockout without it shows no shift after an eye is closed, even at the

9
00:00:43.453 --> 00:00:47.133
normal age. Its neurons still respond to vision.

10
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.573
What failed was the change.

11
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:57.603
Now add diazepam. It makes the GABA receptor respond more strongly to GABA, but it

12
00:00:57.603 --> 00:00:59.423
cannot open the receptor alone.

13
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:04.503
Think of a smoke detector that needs a minimum charge before it sounds.

14
00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:09.283
The knockout's battery is low, and diazepam tops it up.

15
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:12.963
Given during deprivation, it restores the shift.

16
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:18.003
A normal mouse has its window in a few weeks of youth.

17
00:01:19.023 --> 00:01:22.883
Knockouts kept their capacity for a critical period into adulthood.

18
00:01:23.963 --> 00:01:27.503
There, diazepam triggered the window whenever it was given.

19
00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:34.043
In normal young mice, diazepam before the usual opening started the window early.

20
00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:36.083
It also ended early.

21
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:40.997
If the window simply faded, nothing could open it again.

22
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:45.117
Several experiments suggest it is held shut by brakes.

23
00:01:45.977 --> 00:01:50.577
A dense lattice of matrix condenses around many parvalbumin cells.

24
00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:57.377
Myelin signals act through the Nogo receptor, and a protein called Lynx1 damps

25
00:01:57.377 --> 00:01:58.457
nicotinic receptors.

26
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:04.537
In adult rodents, taking away brakes kept plasticity or restored it.

27
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:11.457
Inhibition has to reach a threshold to start the window, and brakes are added to end

28
00:02:11.457 --> 00:02:17.597
it. Boosting inhibition does not shift the cortex by itself, but it lets the cortex

29
00:02:17.597 --> 00:02:19.037
respond to deprivation.

30
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:25.257
In mice and rats, genes, drugs and experience can move both steps.

31
00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:29.397
These results show mechanism, not treatment for people.

32
00:02:29.397 --> 00:02:35.557
So keep this rule: a critical period is a state of a circuit, not an age.
